    "biography": {
      "concept": "Keeps six old hives behind a poor, clean house, in the Hum (Act 3+)",
      "notes": "The Bee-Teller keeps six hives behind a poor, clean house with a low corrugated roof; the hives are older than the house. She is plain, practical and unhurried, without ceremony. She keeps a strip of black cloth for news of the dead, and tells the bees out loud, in full sentences, with the name and the date. She gives thin, sour honey with a four-word instruction (a drop each eye) and thinks the honey is the boring part. Asked about most other things: 'Not mine to say.'"
    }
